@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Stage A: default-off NGAP application snapshot (offline source only)
+
+Baseline: `11c9bbabb69873752500d676f55e0034f6caa5c5` (25.04 policy reference).
+Implemented in this detached isolated worktree. The existing newer checkout and orchestrator are untouched. No compiler, CMake, linker, package installer, image builder, NF binary or live service was invoked.
+
+## API and default-off boundary
+
+`amf_connection_manager` has an optional final `ngap_snapshot::config` constructor argument, default `{}`. No existing application construction site supplies an enabled config. `source::create` returns null before allocation/entropy reads unless enabled and all bounded local/peer metadata is valid. There is no CLI/YAML switch, listener, exporter, periodic timer, network client or live collector integration.
+
+A future reviewed development caller can supply local NF identity and up to 64 unique AMF-index/peer-ID/expected-AMF-name bindings. Metadata is not authentication or peer-UID proof. Source initialization obtains a nonblocking 128-bit random incarnation with no retry/PID/time fallback; entropy failure leaves the source unavailable. Production source/build provenance must bind the exact patch/image separately: the payload reports baseline revision and an explicit uncompiled instrumentation revision.
+
+`request_ngap_application_snapshot()` returns a one-shot ticket or null for disabled, closed, allocation/queue/admission failure. One ticket remains outstanding until all references are released; no second queued request is admitted. No polling loop/retry/wait occurs inside the source. `ngap_snapshot_incarnation()` returns immutable producer identity for an independently reviewed consumer binding. `ticket::inspect(expected_incarnation)` returns an immutable copy with UNKNOWN rows if stale, incomplete, closed, wrong incarnation or superseded by a lifecycle revision. `valid` means a structurally fresh/complete local snapshot, not healthy NGAP or an approved Layer 3 source.
+
+## Authoritative hooks and generation invariants
+
+The setup routine observes the original `amf_connected = success` write after NG Setup outcome. Loss follows the original per-AMF erase; reconnect follows its native setup result and original map insertion; removal observes its native false assignment. Source-only invariant tests compare original assignments/coroutine waits/returns to the patched source. Protocol operations, retries, messages and return values are not intentionally changed; native behavior/ABI equivalence is NOT established without compilation/testing.
+
+Each configured AMF has its own generation and state. Begin/reconnect creates a fresh pending generation; loss, removal-start and unavailable evidence retire previous completion authority. Only a pending matching generation may accept a terminal result. Exact duplicate terminals are idempotent; contradictory terminal results retire the generation and become UNKNOWN. Old setup/removal completions cannot restore/clear state after later loss or reconnect. The older community routines select `map.begin()`; instrumentation checks that the native atomic reference matches the protocol AMF index and emits UNKNOWN when it cannot verify that binding. It does NOT fix or add multi-AMF protocol support to the baseline.
+
+State is local UP/DOWN/UNKNOWN, never HEALTHY service. UP requires explicit NG Setup success, verified native binding and exact reviewed AMF-name match. SCTP creation alone yields no UP evidence. Loss means observed local disconnection, not remote fault onset. An unknown transition timestamp remains unknown; source wall/monotonic stamps record observed local transitions only. Remote AMF restart without a locally observable association change cannot be detected from its name alone; later topology/container/process identity validation remains mandatory.
+
+## Owner-executor and lifetime reasoning
+
+The pinned mapper `lib/cu_cp/cu_cp_executor_mapper.cpp` implements `ctrl_executor()` as a serialized `task_strand`, while N2 RX initially uses a pool. `ngap_repository.cpp` binds NGAP scheduling/timers to `services.cu_cp_executor`; `cu_cp_controller.cpp` gives the same control executor to the manager. Connection-loss handling dispatches onto that executor before the application hook.
+
+The producer is permanently bound to that verified executor at construction. Requesters cannot choose a different executor. Lifecycle writes and deferred source copies are serialized there; readers never traverse the application map or mutable rows. The deferred task captures shared snapshot storage/ticket only, no raw manager/source pointer. Release/acquire publishes a fixed one-shot result. Global revision checks invalidate old results after any lifecycle change. Manager `stop()` closes the source atomically before teardown dispatch; destruction also closes it. Closure never reads owner-only mutable state. Callers must still keep the manager/executor alive through request admission, as with existing manager APIs; pending admitted tasks retain their own storage.
+
+No new mutex, busy wait or allocation occurs in lifecycle hooks. Construction and read admission allocate bounded shared storage, and shared-pointer reclamation/allocator and queue latency still require native profiling; this source review does not prove hard real-time behavior. Custom/enterprise executors not following the verified strand contract are unsupported until separately reviewed. Native TSan/ASan and destruction-race tests remain required.
+
+## Bounds, freshness and snapshot schema
+
+Fixed rows <=64; text <=96 printable bytes; compile-time payload cap <=64KiB; lock-free atomic bool/uint64 are required by static assertions. These assertions have NOT yet been compiled. One deferred read at a time, owner queue age <=10ms, snapshot freshness <=30s, no automatic sampling. Heartbeat sequence advances only on an accepted owner-executed copy; rereading a retained ticket does not refresh it. A delayed owner task cannot fabricate freshness. Missing snapshots mean UNAVAILABLE/UNKNOWN; they do not prove explicit DOWN. Sampling cannot prove instant peer liveness between observations.
+
+Fields: local NF ID; random producer incarnation; base and instrumentation revision; global state revision; owner-produced sequence/observation wall and monotonic timestamps; complete/valid; per-AMF binding and observed AMF name; local state/reason; association generation; terminal pending guard; known/null transition time. There are no subscribers, NAS payloads, authentication material or controller callbacks.
